                The counter above covers every denomination you are likely to meet in a till, and it works in whole rupees because 50 paise coins are too rare in daily takings to matter. The ₹2,000 row is kept for the odd note that still turns up.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing routine -->
        <div class="mt-8 bg-white dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-lg shadow-lg border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8 transition-colors duration-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-4">A Closing-Time Cash Routine That Catches Mistakes</h2>
            <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 space-y-3 mb-4">
                <li><strong>Fix an opening float.</strong> Start each day with a known amount of change (a set number of ₹10, ₹20 and ₹50 notes plus coins) and write it down. Closing cash minus the float is what you actually took in.</li>
                <li><strong>Sort before you count.</strong> Face all notes the same way and stack by denomination. Count each stack by hand, then enter the number of pieces here, not the value, so the multiplication cannot go wrong.</li>
                <li><strong>Compare with sales.</strong> Cash sales for the day should equal total billed sales minus card, UPI and credit sales. The counter total minus the float should match that figure.</li>
                <li><strong>Log the difference.</strong> Record any shortage or excess with the date, the amount and the likely reason (wrong change, an unbilled sale, a note refused as suspect). A running log shows patterns that a single day cannot.</li>
                <li><strong>Prepare the deposit.</strong> Bank pay-in slips ask for the number of notes of each denomination. The breakdown you entered above goes straight onto the slip, so fill the slip before you bundle the notes.</li>
                <li><strong>Move cash off the premises.</strong> Deposit often and keep only the next day's float overnight. Shop insurance policies usually cap the cash they cover, so check your policy schedule.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                The <a href="/blog/cash-handling-tips-small-businesses/" class="text-green-700 dark:text-green-400 underline">cash handling guide</a> covers till discipline, staff handovers and petty cash in more detail. For the day's total in words on a voucher or receipt, paste the amount into the <a href="/number-to-words/" class="text-green-700 dark:text-green-400 underline">number to words converter</a>.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Cash rules -->
        <div class="mt-8 bg-white dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-lg shadow-lg border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8 transition-colors duration-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-4">Cash Rules Every Indian Business Should Know (as of September 2026)</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-6">
                Income tax law limits how much business can be done in cash. These limits were set under Sections 269ST, 40A(3), 269SS and 269T of the Income-tax Act, 1961 and carry forward under the Income-tax Act, 2025, which replaced the 1961 Act from 1 April 2026 and renumbered its sections, so look up the current section number before you cite one.
            </p>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-600">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left border dark:border-gray-500 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">Rule</th>
                            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left border dark:border-gray-500 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">Limit</th>
                            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left border dark:border-gray-500 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">What it means at the counter</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 divide-y divide-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-500">
                        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500 font-semibold">Cash receipts</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">₹2 lakh or more</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">You cannot accept ₹2 lakh or more in cash from one person in a day, for one transaction, or for one event or occasion, even in instalments. The penalty equals the amount received.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500 font-semibold">Cash expenses</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Above ₹10,000 per person per day</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Business payments in cash above this limit are disallowed as an expense (the limit is ₹35,000 for payments to transporters). Pay suppliers by cheque, transfer or UPI.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500 font-semibold">Cash loans and deposits</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">₹20,000 or more</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Loans, deposits and advances against property cannot be accepted or repaid in cash at this level. The penalty equals the amount.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500 font-semibold">PAN on large sales</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Above ₹2 lakh per transaction</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">The customer's PAN must be recorded for any single sale or purchase of goods or services above ₹2 lakh, whatever the payment method.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500 font-semibold">Bank reporting</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">₹10 lakh (savings) or ₹50 lakh (current) in a year</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Banks report cash deposits that cross these totals in a financial year to the Income Tax Department, so keep sales records that explain your deposits.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                These are limits, not advice. A chartered accountant can tell you how they apply to your business, and GST rules on invoicing apply to cash sales in the same way as to any other sale.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Counterfeit notes -->
        <div class="mt-8 bg-white dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-lg shadow-lg border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8 transition-colors duration-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-4">Spotting a Counterfeit Note at the Counter</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4">
                The RBI publishes the security features of every denomination on its website. These are the ones you can check in a second or two without equipment:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-4">
                <li><strong>Watermark:</strong> hold the note to the light to see the Mahatma Gandhi portrait and the denomination numeral in the blank area.</li>
                <li><strong>Security thread:</strong> a windowed thread with the words भारत and RBI runs through the note; on the higher denominations it changes colour when you tilt the note.</li>
                <li><strong>See-through register:</strong> the denomination numeral is printed in halves on the two sides and forms a complete number against the light.</li>
                <li><strong>Raised printing:</strong> on the higher denominations the portrait, the RBI seal and the identification mark near the edge are printed in intaglio and can be felt with a fingertip.</li>
                <li><strong>Number panel:</strong> in the current series the numerals grow from small to large from left to right.</li>
                <li><strong>Micro lettering:</strong> RBI and the denomination value appear in tiny letters near the portrait, visible with a magnifying glass.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                Under RBI rules a bank that receives a counterfeit note impounds it, stamps it and gives you an acknowledgement; you do not get its value back. That is why checking large notes when they are tendered matters more than any check at the bank. A small UV lamp is a cheap addition to a busy counter: genuine notes have optical fibres and number panels that glow under it, and a note that stays dull deserves a closer look.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div class="mt-8 bg-white dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-lg shadow-lg border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8 transition-colors duration-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div class="space-y-4">
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        Which Indian currency notes are in circulation in 2026?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">The RBI issues ₹10, ₹20, ₹50, ₹100, ₹200 and ₹500 notes. The ₹2,000 note was withdrawn from circulation in May 2023 but remains legal tender, and older ₹1, ₹2 and ₹5 notes are still valid even though they are no longer printed in volume.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        Is the ₹2,000 note still valid?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">Yes. The RBI withdrew it from circulation on 19 May 2023, but it continues to be legal tender. Banks stopped exchanging it on 7 October 2023, and it can now be exchanged or deposited through RBI issue offices under the RBI's current rules.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        Are 50 paise coins still legal tender?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">Yes. Coins of 50 paise remain legal tender. Coins of 25 paise and below were withdrawn from 30 June 2011 and are no longer accepted.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        How much cash can a shop accept from one customer?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">Receiving ₹2 lakh or more in cash from one person in a day, for one transaction, or for one event is prohibited under income tax law, and the penalty equals the amount received. Below that limit cash sales are allowed, but the customer's PAN must be recorded for any single sale above ₹2 lakh whatever the payment method.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        How do shopkeepers count cash at the end of the day?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">Sort the notes by denomination, count each stack, and multiply by the face value. Enter those counts here to get the total instantly, compare it with the day's cash sales, and note any shortage or excess before locking up.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        Does this cash counter store my counts?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">Counts are saved only in your own browser so they survive a page refresh. Nothing is sent to a server, and Reset All clears the saved values.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        Can I use this counter to fill a bank deposit slip?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">Yes. Pay-in slips ask for the number of notes of each denomination. Enter the same counts here to check the total before you write it on the slip.</p>
                </details>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</main>

{% block scripts %}
<script>
    function formatIndianCurrency(num) {
        return '₹' + parseFloat(num).toLocaleString('en-IN', { minimumFractionDigits: 0, maximumFractionDigits: 0 });
    }

    function numberToWords(num) {
        if (num === 0) return '';

        const crore = Math.floor(num / 10000000);
        const lakh = Math.floor((num % 10000000) / 100000);
        const thousand = Math.floor((num % 100000) / 1000);
        const hundred = Math.floor((num % 1000) / 100);
        const remainder = num % 100;

        let words = [];
        if (crore > 0) words.push(crore + ' Crore');
        if (lakh > 0) words.push(lakh + ' Lakh');
        if (thousand > 0) words.push(thousand + ' Thousand');
        if (hundred > 0) words.push(hundred + ' Hundred');
        if (remainder > 0) words.push(remainder);

        return words.join(' ') + ' Rupees';
    }

    function calculateTotal() {
        const denominations = {
            note2000: 2000,
            note500: 500,
            note200: 200,
            note100: 100,
            note50: 50,
            note20: 20,
            note10: 10,
            coin20: 20,
            coin10: 10,
            coin5: 5,
            coin2: 2,
            coin1: 1
        };

        let total = 0;
        for (const [id, value] of Object.entries(denominations)) {
            let count = parseInt(document.getElementById(id).value) || 0;
            // Limit invalid or excessive inputs
            if (count > 99999) {
                count = 99999;
                document.getElementById(id).value = 99999;
            }
            if (count < 0) {
                count = 0;
                document.getElementById(id).value = 0;
            }
            total += count * value;
        }

        document.getElementById('totalAmount').textContent = formatIndianCurrency(total);
        document.getElementById('totalInWords').textContent = total > 0 ? numberToWords(total) : '';
    }



    // Calculate on page load
    window.addEventListener('load', function () {
        initAutoSave('cash_counter');
        calculateTotal();
    });
</script>
{% endblock %}
